# Avoid images that contain text

Avoid images that contain text. Write the words as text on the dashboard, or use one of the [alternatives on this page](#possible-alternatives).

Text inside an image is hard to read for people who:

- use a screen reader
- copy and paste text, for example into a translation app
- highlight text to keep their place
- make text bigger
- change text colours, for example with a high contrast setting

A picture of a reading is also out of date as soon as the reading changes. Do not use an image of text as decoration either.

## Show text as written content, not as images

You rarely need an image just to show some words. Do not use an image to show:

- a reading or a state, such as “Front door: locked”
- the steps of an automation, as a screenshot of the automation editor
- a notification from the Companion app, as a screenshot
- the bin collection days, as a photograph of the council leaflet

If you use an image like this anyway, put text next to it that gives the same meaning. For the bins, a Markdown card can list the next collection day for each bin.

## If text is not the focus of an image

If text in an image is not the point of the image, it can stay. For example:

- a photograph of the boiler, where the maker’s name is on the front
- a photograph of the hall cupboard, with labels on the storage boxes behind the hub
- an illustration of a kitchen, with a recipe book on the shelf

Write alt text that gives the meaning of the image, not the words that happen to be in it. If people need the image to do the task, also put the same meaning in text next to it.

Make sure the image helps people and is not just decoration.

## Essential text in images

In a few cases, an image may contain text. The text is essential when:

- the text is there on purpose and gives the image its meaning
- the image would lose that meaning without the text

This means you cannot give the same information as text alone.

The text must be part of what people need to know. For example:

- what an error code looks like on the boiler’s display, so people can match it to what they see
- how the setup code is printed on the label of a new device, so people can find it

Text can also be essential when it shows an identity in a particular way. For example:

- a household crest with the family name drawn into it
- a hand-lettered house name on a sign

### What to do with essential text in images

Decide whether you need the image, and whether its text is essential. Look at the [alternatives](#possible-alternatives) first.

If you use an image with essential text, give it alt text that gives the meaning of the image and its text. For the boiler, the alt text might be “Boiler display showing the error code F28”. Write the error code and what to do in text as well. [Alt text](images-alt-text.html) explains how to write it.

## Possible alternatives

If you cannot replace an image of text with written content, use one of these instead. Use them even when the text is essential, because they work better for people who change how pages look.

Text over an image needs the same contrast as any other text, against every part of the image it covers. [Accessibility](../accessibility/index.html) sets the contrast target.

### Show real text over an image

Put real text on top of the image. In Home Assistant, a Picture elements card can place a state label, such as the hall temperature, on a floor plan as text.

Check the contrast on a phone, a tablet and a wall panel, and when zoomed in.

### Use a vector graphic

Draw the image and its text as an SVG file. Text in an SVG stays sharp when people zoom in. Text in a PNG or JPEG goes blurred and can become unreadable.

Check the contrast in the SVG too.

## Text in charts, diagrams and complex images

Making a chart, diagram or complex image accessible takes work. First ask whether a table or a few sentences would do.

Give the image short alt text that says what it shows and why. Then give the detail in a longer description. Show the description, or a button that opens it, to everyone, not only to screen reader users.

A chart drawn with real HTML or SVG can be accessible, but only if its data is also there as text. [Charts](brand-data-charts.html#bg-dat-07) explains how to give a chart a text alternative.

### Floor plans and diagrams

Give every floor plan and diagram a short visible text description. Where it holds data, give the same facts in text.

The Picture elements card has no alt text option. Next to the floor plan, add a Markdown card that lists the rooms and devices that matter.

